 operator credentials, snapshots and hashes the policy, and starts a durable kernel owner.
+Every Docker call selects the supplied context explicitly. The owner record pins
+its endpoint, daemon ID, operating system and architecture; restart refuses a
+changed binding. This does not change the active context. Choose the context
+in the Linux owner environment: a host-side Colima context name is not implicitly
+available inside its guest. An older owner without this binding needs an explicit
+qualified migration that preserves its state.
+The runtime server name and version come from the pinned signed manifest.
+Restart checks those exact arguments before signaling the owner. The kernel's
+default server name can differ from the reviewed resource identity and prevent
+authenticated preparation; overriding the manifest to hide that mismatch is
+not a qualified migration.
 Use `--policy /absolute/selected-policy.yaml` for a separately qualified policy,
 such as the explicit-confirmation qualification policy. Restart rejects changes
 to that snapshot.
